    "\n",
    "Name: Dylan Truong\t\tPID: A19151259\n",
    "Name: Paco Yan \t\t    PID: A19140337\n",
    "Name: Diego Jimenez\t\tPID: A19131950\n",
    "Name: Xavier Lu         PID: A18632134\n",
    "Name: Pan               PID: A18484234\n",
    "\n",
    "## Project Proposal\n",
    "RESEARCH QUESTION\n",
    "How has the rapid expansion of the Artificial Intelligence industry—specifically the shift in manufacturing focus toward server-grade memory (HBM) and the rise of local AI applications—affected the pricing trends of consumer-grade RAM?\n",
    "\n",
    "## BACKGROUND & PRIOR WORK:\n",
    "The semiconductor memory market historically follows cyclical \"boom and bust\" patterns driven by supply and demand. Typically, as manufacturing technology matures, yields improve, and prices for consumer RAM (DDR4/DDR5) decrease over time. However, the unprecedented explosion of Generative AI since late 2022 has introduced a new variable. Major manufacturers like Samsung and SK Hynix have reportedly shifted significant production capacity away from standard consumer DRAM to focus on High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) required for AI GPUs, potentially creating a \"supply shock\" for the consumer market.\n",
    "Prior work in this domain often focuses on general hardware price trends, such as the impact of cryptocurrency mining on GPU prices (a demand-side shock). There is less existing analysis on how the AI server boom creates a \"crowding out\" effect for the average PC user's memory components. Our project aims to fill this gap by analyzing whether the AI boom has disrupted the expected historical depreciation rates of computer memory.\n",
    "Reference 1: The Industry Evidence (Tom's Hardware, November 2025))\n",
    "Title: \"Bewildered enthusiasts decry memory price increases of 100% or more — the AI RAM squeeze is finally starting to hit PC builders where it hurts\"\n",
    "\n",
    "## Reference 2: The Historical Comparison (Prior Work) (Kaggle, Maharshi Pandya)\n",
    "Title: \"Cryptocurrency Historical Prices Dataset\"\n",
    "4. Hypothesis\n",
    "We hypothesize that RAM pricing follows a logarithmic decay curve following the release of a new generation, taking approximately 18-24 months to reach price per gigabyte parity with the previous generation.\n",
    "Reasoning: New manufacturing processes (like DDR5) initially have low yields and high R&D recovery costs. As production scales and yields improve, prices drop rapidly before stabilizing. We expect high-speed, low-latency kits to retain value longer than standard \"budget\" kits.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. Data\n",
    "To answer this, we need a dataset containing historical pricing for computer memory modules.\n",
    "Ideal Variables:\n",
    "Date: Weekly observation intervals.\n",
    "Product Name: Manufacturer and model (e.g., G.Skill Ripjaws V).\n",
    "Price: USD.\n",
    "Capacity: Total size in GB (e.g., 16GB, 32GB).\n",
    "Type: DDR generation (DDR3, DDR4, DDR5).\n",
    "Speed: Frequency in MHz (e.g., 3200, 3600, 6000).\n",
    "CAS Latency: Timing (e.g., CL16, CL18).\n",
    "Sources: We plan to use historical data scraped from PCPartPicker (often available via Kaggle archives) or potentially use the CamelCamelCamel API for Amazon price history if direct scraping is blocked. We aim for at least ~1000 observations covering the transition periods of DDR4 (2014) and DDR5 (2021).\n",
    "Storage: The data would be stored in JupyterLab for charts and graphs to make comparisons and correlations. Allow for cleaning data more efficiently. \n",
    "\n",
    "## ETHICS & PRIVACY\n",
    "Data Biases: Our pricing data will likely be sourced from major US retailers (like Amazon or Newegg). This creates a geographic bias, as it may not reflect the hardware accessibility issues faced by consumers in other parts of the world.\n",
    "Economic Implications: If our hypothesis is correct, it highlights a form of \"technological gentrification,\" where enterprise demand for AI makes basic computing hardware more expensive for students and low-income individuals. Discussing this aligns with the course goal of understanding the social impact of data.\n",
    "Privacy: The data involves public product prices and stock market figures. No personally identifiable information (PII) of individuals will be collected or analyzed.\n",
